What if everything you've been told about AI knowledge management is exactly backwards?

In April twenty twenty-six, Andrej Karpathy published a single markdown file on GitHub Gist. No product launch. No startup announcement. Just one file called LLM Wiki, dot M D. And in it was an idea so simple it almost sounds obvious in retrospect: stop retrieving your knowledge every single time you need it, and start compiling it once instead.

This is the way software has worked for decades. You write source code once, compile it into a binary, and run that binary whenever you need it. You do not recompile on every execution. Karpathy says treat your knowledge the same way. Your PDFs, articles, and meeting notes are the source code. The wiki that an AI builds from them is the compiled binary. Every question you ask reads the wiki, not the raw documents. You are no longer recompiling your entire knowledge base from scratch every single time you ask a question.

The architecture has three clean layers. Layer one is your raw sources, your curated collection of articles, papers, and notes. Immutable. The AI reads them but never touches the originals. This is deliberate: you can always rebuild the wiki from scratch if something goes wrong. Layer two is the wiki itself, a directory of markdown files the AI maintains completely. Entity pages for people and organisations, concept pages for ideas, an index file, and a change log. You read the wiki. The AI writes it. Layer three is the schema, a configuration file that turns a generic AI agent into a disciplined wiki maintainer. It defines page structures, ingestion workflows, and formatting conventions. As Karpathy put it: Obsidian is the IDE, the LLM is the programmer, the wiki is the codebase.

Three operations power the whole system. Ingest: you drop a source into the raw folder, the AI reads it, writes a summary page, and touches ten to fifteen related pages, updating cross-links, flagging contradictions. A single article becomes a ripple of updates across your entire knowledge base. Query: you ask a question and the AI reads the pre-synthesised wiki and answers with citations. Good answers get filed back as new pages, so your explorations become permanent knowledge. Lint: periodically the AI audits the whole wiki for contradictions, orphan pages with no inbound links, and concepts mentioned but missing their own page. The wiki stays healthy because the AI does the bookkeeping no human ever wants to do.

This pattern has a deeper lineage most people do not know about. In nineteen forty-five, Vannevar Bush published an essay called As We May Think in The Atlantic. He proposed the Memex, a personal, curated knowledge store where the connections between documents are as valuable as the documents themselves. Bush's vision never really got built because nobody wants to do the bookkeeping: updating cross-references, keeping summaries current, noting when new data contradicts old claims. That is exactly what Karpathy's pattern automates. The tedious part of maintaining a knowledge base is not the reading or the thinking. It is the filing. The AI handles the filing.

I covered a similar system in episode one oh one, the GBrain system developed by Garry Tan, which indexes one hundred forty-six thousand pages, twenty-five thousand people, and five thousand companies. Same compile-once philosophy at serious scale. In episode thirty-seven, the Hermes Agent's LLM Wiki skill applies the identical pattern with Obsidian as the viewer. The convergence is not coincidence. The most effective personal knowledge systems are moving from retrieval-first to compilation-first architectures.

The Señor Freedom angle here is the part that matters most to me. Your knowledge base lives on your machine. No cloud dependency. No vendor lock-in. Your data stays yours. The raw sources are immutable files on your filesystem. The wiki is markdown you can read with any text editor. The schema is a configuration file you control entirely. If your AI provider disappears tomorrow, you still have the wiki. You can recompile it with a different provider. This is sovereignty over your own knowledge, and in a world where every platform wants to host your thinking inside their walled garden, that matters more than convenience.

So the question was never which tool reads your notes the fastest. It is who compiles them — you, deliberately, once, with a structure you control, or a system that profits from keeping that structure just out of your reach. That is the part you were told backwards. The model was never the asset. The knowledge base you build to feed it is. Own the compile step, or spend the rest of the decade renting your own mind back from whoever did.